<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CAPACITOR_DEBUG</key>
	<string>$(CAPACITOR_DEBUG)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Flock</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UILaunchStoryboardName</key>
	<string>LaunchScreen</string>
	<key>UIMainStoryboardFile</key>
	<string>Main</string>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Portrait only. Every screen in the app is laid out as a single portrait
	     column (bottom tab bar, full-height chat, bottom sheets), so declaring
	     landscape advertised a layout the app has never supported. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- When-in-use only, and that is the whole story: the app reads location
	     through the web navigator.geolocation API for nearby venues, in-flock
	     live share, and the SOS alert. No always-authorization API is reachable
	     any more (the unused @capacitor/geolocation plugin was removed, which is
	     what forced the ITMS-90683 always-usage string that used to sit here),
	     and no location background mode is declared. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Flock uses your location to show nearby venues and crowd levels, share live location with your group while a flock is active, and send your location to trusted contacts when you tap SOS.</string>
	<!-- Camera. Three ways in, all started by an explicit tap:
	       1. the in-app viewfinder (App.js `openCameraViewfinder`, getUserMedia,
	          opened from the flock chat composer and the DM composer),
	       2. the friend-code scanner (App.js `startQrScanner`, html5-qrcode,
	          which decodes to `addFriendByCode`), and
	       3. "Take Photo" on any of the three <input type="file" accept="image/*">
	          pickers, which is the path that also reaches the profile picture.
	          That third one is easy to miss when reading the code, because no
	          line in the client mentions the camera: iOS puts the option in the
	          file-input action sheet on its own. (The venue logo is no longer a
	          picker: it is chosen from the linked Google listing's own photos,
	          so neither the camera nor the library ever reaches it.)
	     Every getUserMedia call passes { audio: false }, so no microphone string
	     belongs here.

	     These strings used to promise "stories". No client code renders, posts
	     or reads a story: App.js states it outright at the `story: 'none'`
	     takedown entry, and services/api.js `getStories` has zero callers. The
	     claim was removed from the privacy policy and the community guidelines
	     in f8dad66, and the permission prompt was the last place it survived.
	     If a story UI ever ships, these strings change with it. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Flock uses the camera when you take a photo to send in a flock chat or a direct message or to set as your profile picture, and when you scan a friend's Flock code to add them.</string>
	<!-- Photo library, read only. Three "choose a photo" file inputs, all
	     <input type="file" accept="image/*"> handled in the web view:
	     profile picture (`handlePhotoUpload`), flock chat (`handleChatImageSelect`),
	     DM (`handleDmImageSelect`). The venue logo is NOT one of them: it is
	     picked from the venue's own Google listing photos, never the library.
	     Nothing in the app ever writes an image back to the library, so there is
	     deliberately no NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Flock uses your photo library when you pick a photo to send in a flock chat or a direct message or to set as your profile picture.</string>
	<!-- Contacts, read only, and only phone numbers.

	     WHAT ASKS FOR IT. One thing:
	     frontend/src/services/contacts.js `readContactPhoneNumbers`, which calls
	     Contacts.requestPermissions() and then Contacts.getContacts() with the
	     projection { phones: true } and nothing else. No name, no email, no
	     image, no note, no postal address leaves the phone. There is no
	     createContact or deleteContact call anywhere in the client, which is why
	     this is the read string and there is nothing here about writing.

	     WHEN IT FIRES, and this is the part that is easy to get wrong. iOS asks
	     for contacts ONCE per install: a denial is permanent until the person
	     opens Settings, and almost nobody does. So the prompt must never appear
	     on launch, on sign-up, or on any screen the user did not ask for. The
	     only call site is behind an explicit tap on the "Find friends from your
	     contacts" control inside Add friends, after a screen that has already
	     said in words what will be sent and what will be kept. Anything that
	     fires this earlier is spending the one prompt this app gets on a moment
	     when the user has no reason to say yes. (The notification prompt is the
	     cautionary example: it is spent at cold start.)

	     iOS 18 LIMITED ACCESS is a success, not a fallback. The plugin reports
	     `limited` when the person chose which contacts to share, getContacts
	     then returns only those, and the app treats that as a completed sync
	     rather than nagging for full access. Do not add a "grant full access"
	     prompt on top of it.

	     WHY NOT THE PICKER INSTEAD. CNContactPickerViewController needs no
	     authorization at all, which would be better, but the plugin's
	     pickContact() requests full contacts permission before presenting it
	     (see isContactsPermissionGranted in
	     node_modules/@capacitor-community/contacts/ios/Sources/ContactsPlugin/ContactsPlugin.swift)
	     and returns one contact per call. So it costs the same prompt and
	     delivers less. If that plugin ever presents the picker without asking,
	     the picker becomes the better path and this key can go. -->
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Flock checks your contacts against the people already using Flock so you can add the friends you already have. Only phone numbers are sent, they are not stored, and nothing else from a contact card leaves your phone.</string>
	<!-- Exactly the custom schemes the app can be asked to open, and no more.

	     The bill-split screen is the only place that opens one. The authority is
	     backend/routes/billing.js, which builds `deepLink` for exactly two
	     methods: venmo://paycharge (also used by /venmo-link) and
	     cashapp://cash.app/pay. Its third method, Zelle, is built with
	     `deepLink: null` and shows written instructions instead, because Zelle
	     lives inside each bank's own app and has no shared scheme. `zelle` and
	     `cash-app` were both listed here and neither is ever constructed, so
	     they are gone. tel/sms/mailto are system schemes and need no entry.

	     Be precise about what this key does and does not do here, because it is
	     easy to assume it is load-bearing and it currently is not. Opening the
	     link does NOT depend on it: window.open() on a non-http URL reaches
	     Capacitor's WebViewDelegationHandler, which calls
	     UIApplication.open(url) directly, and -open: needs no declaration. Only
	     -canOpenURL: consults this list, and nothing in the app calls it.

	     It is kept, accurate and trimmed, because it is the list -canOpenURL:
	     would consult on the day something can call it. Nothing can today:
	     @capacitor/app 8.x exposes getInfo, getState, getLaunchUrl, exitApp,
	     minimizeApp and the listeners, and no canOpenUrl, and no other installed
	     plugin bridges it either. So this array is still not load-bearing, and
	     the silent-failure bug it used to describe was fixed WITHOUT it.

	     What that bug was: with no Venmo app installed, -open: fails silently
	     and the Pay button did nothing, because App.js reached for `webLink`
	     only when `deepLink` was null, never when opening the deep link failed.
	     App.js `attemptPaymentHandoff` now opens the deep link inside the tap
	     and races the app going to the background (visibilitychange to hidden,
	     pagehide) against a 1.5s timer. Background wins: the wallet took the
	     link and nothing else happens. The timer wins: the payer gets a sheet
	     with the web route on a fresh tap. Read the comment above that function
	     before changing either side; the timing and the choice of signals are
	     the whole correctness argument.

	     If a canOpenURL bridge is ever added, this array stops being decorative
	     and the race can become a straight question. Either way, keep these two
	     entries in step with billing.js. -->
	<key>LSApplicationQueriesSchemes</key>
	<array>
		<string>venmo</string>
		<string>cashapp</string>
	</array>
	<!-- No UIBackgroundModes. `remote-notification` used to be declared, and
	     nothing earns it: backend/services/firebaseService.js sends every push
	     with 'apns-push-type': 'alert' and an aps payload of sound + thread-id
	     only. There is no content-available anywhere, so iOS never wakes the app
	     in the background and the mode was a capability the app does not use.
	     Alert push is unaffected by removing it; what push needs is the
	     aps-environment entitlement, which App.entitlements still declares.
	     If a silent/data push is ever added, add `content-available` there and
	     this array back here in the same change. -->
	<!-- No NSAppTransportSecurity key, i.e. no ATS exception of any kind, and
	     that is correct rather than an oversight. The web layer is served from
	     the bundle over capacitor://localhost and every origin it then talks to
	     is https: the Railway API, MapTiler, Google Places, Firebase, PostHog.
	     Nothing in frontend/ loads an http:// URL. If an exception is ever
	     needed, scope it to the one domain with NSExceptionDomains rather than
	     reaching for NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. -->

	<!-- ONE URL scheme, and it is not a deep link. Google's iOS SDK completes
	     sign-in by redirecting to the REVERSED form of the app's iOS OAuth
	     client id, so that scheme has to be declared here or the sheet opens
	     and never comes back. Nothing in the app builds or receives such a URL
	     itself; it exists only as the callback GoogleSignIn hands to
	     ASWebAuthenticationSession.

	     THE VALUE BELOW IS THE REAL REVERSED CLIENT ID, set in 8e14b96, and it
	     is not a placeholder any more. This comment said the opposite for two
	     days after that commit landed, which is worse than saying nothing: the
	     next reader's instruction was to replace a working callback scheme, and
	     a wrong one here is a Google sheet that opens and never comes back.
	     Change it only if the iOS OAuth client itself is reissued.

	     Where it comes from, if it ever has to be reissued: Google Cloud
	     console → APIs & Services → Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth
	     client ID → Application type "iOS" → Bundle ID com.flockcorp.flock. The
	     client id it issues reads <digits>-<hash>.apps.googleusercontent.com;
	     the reversed form, which is what goes in the <string> below, is the same
	     thing with the two halves swapped.
	     The UNreversed client id also has to be set as REACT_APP_GOOGLE_IOS_-
	     CLIENT_ID in the Codemagic `flock_web` env group; the two are read
	     together by src/components/auth/useGoogleAuth.js, and while the env
	     var is unset the Google button hides itself on native rather than
	     shipping a button that cannot work.

	     This is deliberately NOT a `flock://` deep-link entry. Nothing in the
	     repo emits a flock: URL (push payloads carry relative paths, invites
	     carry https links), so declaring one would be a capability no code
	     uses — see the associated-domains note below, which is unchanged. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.flockcorp.flock.googlesignin</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>com.googleusercontent.apps.1012360079798-1elq821nnbqjun84b857ms01h7irjraq</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- No com.apple.developer.associated-domains, so
	     iOS currently delivers NO universal link to this app. Both halves of
	     services/pushNavigation.js `intentFromUrl` are therefore unreachable on
	     device today: the custom-scheme branch (flock://flock/123) and the
	     universal-link paths (/f/123, /dm/45). Push taps still route correctly,
	     because those come through the FCM notification payload rather than a
	     URL.

	     Neither is added here on purpose, and the reasons are different.

	     A `flock://` CFBundleURLTypes entry stays out, and the array above is
	     not one: that entry is Google's OAuth callback, which the SDK itself
	     produces and consumes. An app-owned scheme would be a declaration
	     nothing produces — a repo-wide search for a flock scheme URL finds this
	     comment, services/pushNavigation.js's own comment, and the abandoned
	     mobile/ React Native port, and nothing that builds one.
	     backend/services/firebaseService.js deepLinkPath emits relative paths
	     (/?flock=12, /?dm=45, /?tab=you); routes/flocks.js emits
	     PUBLIC_WEB_URL/i/<token>; emailService.js emits https links. Declaring
	     an entry point no push, no email, no invite and no web page ever hands
	     out is the "capability declared that no code uses" defect this shell's
	     test file exists to catch, and iosShellConfigMatchesCode.test.js pins
	     the absence of a flock scheme. It also would not move the problem
	     people actually hit, which is the next paragraph. Add it in the SAME
	     change as the first thing that emits such a URL, not before.

	     Associated domains cannot be fixed from this repo at all: the
	     entitlement only signs once the Associated Domains capability is
	     enabled on App ID com.flockcorp.flock AND an apple-app-site-association
	     file is served from https://www.flockcorp.com/.well-known/ with the
	     correct Team ID. Adding the entitlement before those two exist fails
	     the archive. This is the half that costs growth: an invite link
	     (https://www.flockcorp.com/i/<token>, shared via navigator.share) opens
	     the web invite page in Safari even on a phone with the app installed.

	     One more thing has to land with that capability, and it is code, not
	     configuration: services/pushNavigation.js `intentFromUrl` parses /f/:id
	     and /dm/:id and does NOT parse /i/<token>, so an invite universal link
	     would be delivered and then resolve to no intent at all. The app also
	     has no route that redeems an invite token; today that is the guest web
	     page. Turning the capability on without that work would replace "opens
	     Safari" with "opens the app on whatever screen it was already on". -->

	<!-- Export compliance, verified against the bundle on 2026-08-14. The only
	     cryptography this app performs is standard TLS supplied by the OS: the
	     web layer talks HTTPS/WSS through WKWebView's stack, and every native
	     plugin (Sign in with Apple, Firebase Messaging, RevenueCat) rides OS
	     frameworks that do the same. What was checked, so the next reader does
	     not have to re-derive it: no crypto library appears in frontend
	     package.json dependencies, no file under frontend/src calls WebCrypto
	     or any cipher API, and the one lockfile hit (crypto-random-string) is
	     react-scripts build tooling that never ships. That usage is exempt, so
	     false is the correct value, and declaring it here answers App Store
	     Connect's export question once instead of on every single upload.

	     France: the French declaration obligation attaches to NON exempt
	     encryption only, so this value also means no French paperwork exists
	     for this app. The day someone adds encryption of their own (an E2E
	     chat layer, a custom cipher, a bundled crypto library) this key flips,
	     the per-build question returns, and the France answer has to be
	     revisited in the same change. iosShellConfigMatchesCode.test.js pins
	     the premise: it fails if a crypto dependency or a WebCrypto call ever
	     appears, which is the moment this comment stops being true.
	     Submission-day answers live in APP-STORE-SUBMISSION.md at repo root. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
